    "done_when": "Decision, already made by §2: the six DISPATCH_DETAIL_FIELDS live in the run's dispatch-detail.json for both doors, because every pointer write passes through runs._split_dispatch_detail. tests/test_a_queued_launch_keeps_the_run_record.py compares the direct door's stored pointer, re-read from disk, with the queued launch's, each merged with runs.read_dispatch_detail, and reads argv through runs.dispatch_field. If the queued launch's stored detail then lacks a field the direct door stores, that is a real defect: fix it in reckon/crew/queue_admission.py's launch path, not in the test, and say so. Both test_a_refused_then_launched_request_carries_the_launch_record and test_the_launched_record_carries_every_key_a_direct_dispatch_writes pass at the head and fail at the dispatch base. Negative control: make the queued launch drop argv before its pointer write, and the key-parity case fails. The armed suite command, resolved per revision with promotion_records._scoped_suite_command and run literally, adds no failure at the head against the base with the final test file copied into the base tree. The test files importing reckon.crew.queue_admission add no failure against the base, run in place on the fleet node with -n 4 --timeout 120, one arm at a time, each command kept under about 8 minutes. ruff check and ruff format --check add no findings on the staged paths. Every gate log starts with its revision and literal command and ends in a bare EXIT= line",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Make the queued-launch record tests compare what each dispatch door stored, read through runs.read_dispatch_detail, instead of a direct dispatch's in-memory return",
